# Local development only. `wrangler dev` reads `workers/.dev.vars`; that file is git-ignored and
# must never be committed. Copy this file to `.dev.vars` and fill it in.
#
# Everything here points at STAGING. Do not paste production keys into a file on a laptop that
# `wrangler dev` will run with the service role key — that is the one credential in this project
# that can rewrite every match row, and a dev box is not where it belongs.

APP_ENV=development
SUPABASE_URL=https://REPLACE-WITH-STAGING-REF.supabase.co
SUPABASE_PROJECT_REF=REPLACE-WITH-STAGING-REF
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=REPLACE-WITH-STAGING-PUBLISHABLE-KEY

# SECRET — Supabase dashboard → Settings → API → service_role (a.k.a. "secret key").
# Used only by supabaseAdmin(); grep `supabaseAdmin(` to see every place RLS is bypassed.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# SECRET — Settings → API → JWT Secret. Verifies the HS256 access token the SPA sends (middleware/auth.ts).
SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET=

# SECRET — Cloudflare Turnstile widget secret. Leave blank in dev: verifyTurnstile() skips the check.
TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=

# Exact origins allowed for CORS. `*` is rejected by src/env.ts on purpose; a `null` origin
# (sandboxed iframe, file://) is refused by the middleware, not implicitly allowed.
ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5000,http://127.0.0.1:5000

# ── Phase 5 · push notifications ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Nothing here is required to boot the Worker. With these unset, `transportFor()` returns the mock
# transport: jobs are created, fanned out and recorded, and no message leaves the account. That is the
# intended state for a laptop, and the reason the local default is "silent" rather than "error".

# Not a secret — the *Cloudflare-side* id of the Firebase project whose FCM v1 API you will call. It is
# not the `projectId` in the SPA's Firebase config; those are the same string only when nobody has
# renamed anything, so the Worker reads its own variable (workers/src/env.ts).
FCM_PROJECT_ID=

# SECRET — the downloaded service-account JSON, pasted verbatim onto one line (or set with
# `npx wrangler secret put FCM_SERVICE_ACCOUNT`, which is the only way it reaches a real environment).
# It contains a private key: the pre-commit source scan fails a build that embeds one, and no route ever
# echoes it. Never put it in wrangler.toml, an issue, or a screenshot.
FCM_SERVICE_ACCOUNT=

# Tuning, all optional. Audiences above NOTIFICATIONS_MAX_AUDIENCE are refused rather than truncated;
# the reminder lead time is minutes before kick-off, where 0 turns reminders off; FCM_TIMEOUT_MS bounds
# one HTTP send so a hang cannot consume the queue's visibility window.
NOTIFICATIONS_MAX_AUDIENCE=50000
NOTIFICATIONS_REMINDER_LEAD_MINUTES=15
FCM_TIMEOUT_MS=8000
# Only needed when a push tap may land outside the SPA's own origin (an email link, a native app).
NOTIFICATIONS_LINK_BASE=

# ── Phase 6 · media on R2 ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# No secrets, by design. The bucket is a *binding* (`[[r2_buckets]]` in workers/wrangler.toml, read as
# `env.MEDIA_BUCKET`), so `wrangler dev` reaches the dev bucket with the Cloudflare login you already
# have and there is no key to store here. If a media access key ever appears in this file, the phase was
# implemented wrong — see the note in scripts/check-secrets.mjs.

# Ceiling for one uploaded file, in bytes. The per-kind caps in workers/src/lib/mediaPolicy.ts are
# tighter and are the ones a user sees named in an error; this is the outer bound.
MEDIA_MAX_BYTES=26214400

# ── Phase 7 · advertising ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The two queues and the Durable Object are *bindings* declared in workers/wrangler.toml
# (`[[queues.producers]]`, `[[queues.consumers]]`, `[[durable_objects.bindings]]`), not variables set here —
# `wrangler dev` provisions them locally under `.wrangler/state`. Only queue *names* appear as vars, and they
# belong to wrangler.toml's `[vars]` block so a deploy cannot accidentally point at another environment's queue.

# SECRET — the HMAC input for the per-viewer measurement reference (`POST /advertising/viewer-key`). Set with
# `npx wrangler secret put AD_VIEWER_KEY_SECRET`. Unset, the Worker derives the key from SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET
# instead: it works, but rotating one credential then rotates the other. Rotating this value invalidates every
# key ever issued, which is the entire implementation of "delete this viewer's history" — no table holds a
# viewer identity, so there is nothing to forget, and no delete endpoint to abuse.
AD_VIEWER_KEY_SECRET=<set>

# ── Phase 9 · observability ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# No new secrets and no new bindings: metrics are written through the same Supabase client, log lines go to
# stdout for the platform to collect, and health rows live in `public.system_health`.

# Volume of structured request logs: off | errors | slow | all. The default is `errors` — every failure and
# every slow request, no healthy 200s — because the rollup tables already hold the counting and a raw request
# log is the one thing this phase refused to build. The field list is closed (docs/OBSERVABILITY_ARCHITECTURE.md
# §3): nothing you set here adds a field to a log line.
LOG_MODE=<set>

# There is deliberately no `ENVIRONMENT` variable: `isProduction(env)`, `envName(env)` and `/api/health` all
# read `APP_ENV` above. Two names for one fact is how an environment ends up production-shaped with dev keys.
